      "problem": "Factor $p^2 - 10p + 25.$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\np^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} p + a^2 =  (p-{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\np^2 - 10p + 25.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-10p+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 5p+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{5}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
